          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "For a new blood pressure medicine, the guidance describes **monthly review until the blood pressure target is reached**. Once the readings are stable, review every **three to six months**. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*, major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary.)\n\nThe same guidance says that after starting a medicine that acts on the renin-angiotensin system, a check of **kidney function and potassium** is part of the follow-up. Those checks are also part of the monthly review. It also says to review how consistently the medicine is being taken.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give the schedule for every blood pressure medicine, and they do not say how soon a first check should happen. If you have started or are considering a medicine, your prescriber can tell you the schedule that fits your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
